# Artifact schema (the central deliverable)

This is the most heavily evaluated part of the whole project. The artifact is
a typed, serializable, agent-invocable **capability** — not just a step list.

## Top-level shape

```ts
CapabilityArtifact {
  schemaVersion: "1.0"
  id: "member.read_savings_balance"       // stable name, callable by an agent
  version: "1.2.0"                        // semver of this capability
  status: "draft" | "approved" | "deprecated"   // unapproved artifacts must
                                                 // never run unattended
  name, description

  target: {
    appId: "corebank-lite"                // vendor-product identity, the key
                                           // for cross-tenant reuse
    appVersionRange?: ">=2.1 <3.0"
    tenantId?: string                     // absent = base artifact
    entryPoint: "{{baseUrl}}/members/search"   // templated, never hardcoded
  }

  surface: { kind: "web" | "legacy-web" | "desktop", capabilities: [...] }

  inputs:  ParamSpec[]
  outputs: OutputSpec[]
  steps:   Step[]
  successCheckpoint: Assertion
  outcomes: OutcomeRule[]                 // declarative business outcomes
  recoveries: RecoveryRule[]              // declarative recoverable conditions

  policy: {
    allowedOrigins: string[]
    allowedActions: ActionKind[]
    maxSteps: number
    highestRisk: "safe" | "reversible" | "irreversible"
  }

  provenance: { discoveryRunId, model, promptVersion, recordedAt, recordedBy }
  health?: { replays, successes, lastVerifiedAt, fallbackTierHistogram }
}
```

## Inputs / outputs (sensitivity-tagged)

```ts
ParamSpec  { name, type: "string"|"number"|"boolean"|"enum", enum?, required,
             description, sensitivity: "public"|"pii"|"secret", example? }
OutputSpec { name, type, description, sensitivity,
             source: { stepId, extract } }
```
`sensitivity != "public"` fields are stored only as parameter references,
never as literal values. `example` is allowed only for `public` fields.

## Step

```ts
Step {
  id: "s03"
  intent: "Enter the member ID into the search box"   // human-readable, for review
  action: "navigate"|"click"|"fill"|"select"|"press"|"extract"|"waitFor"|"assert"
  target?: Locator
  value?: { kind: "literal", value } | { kind: "param", name: "memberId" }
  extractAs?: string
  precondition?: Assertion
  postcondition?: Assertion              // never assume a click worked
  wait?: { until: "networkIdle"|"assertion", timeoutMs, retries }
  risk: "safe" | "reversible" | "irreversible"
}
```

## Locator — the part most likely to get probed in review

```ts
Locator {
  frame?: string[]                       // iframe/frameset path, required for legacy
  primary:   Strategy
  fallbacks: Strategy[]                  // ordered ladder
  verify?: { role?, nameContains?, textMatches? }   // re-check after resolving,
                                                     // prevents "matched the
                                                     // wrong element and kept going"
}

Strategy =
  | { by: "role",        role, name, nameMatch: "exact"|"contains" }   // ← first choice,
                                                                        //   portable across
                                                                        //   web/desktop
  | { by: "label",       text }
  | { by: "textAnchor",  anchorText, direction: "right"|"below", nth } // ← for legacy
                                                                        //   table layouts
  | { by: "css",         selector }
  | { by: "domPath",     path }                                       // ← last resort
  | { by: "coordinates", x, y, relativeTo: "viewport"|"anchor" }      // ← desktop /
                                                                        //   no-DOM fallback
```

**Why `role + name` is ranked first:** it is the one concept shared between
the Chromium accessibility tree, Windows UIA (ControlType + Name), and
macOS AX (AXRole + AXTitle). This is what lets an artifact's steps survive a
swap of the underlying `Surface` implementation without rewriting locators.

**Why `verify` exists:** the risk of a fallback ladder isn't "can't find the
element," it's "matched the wrong element and kept executing against it."
After resolving a candidate handle, re-check its role/text; if it fails,
treat it as unresolved and move to the next tier — never silently proceed on
a mismatched element.

**Fallback-tier reporting is the drift signal.** Every resolution records
which tier (primary vs. which fallback) succeeded into
`health.fallbackTierHistogram`. A rising rate of non-primary hits for a given
tenant/app version is the detection signal for UI drift — not a diff against
a stored screenshot.

## Assertion / Outcome / Recovery (declarative — no model needed at replay time)

```ts
Assertion =
  | { kind: "visible", locator }
  | { kind: "textPresent", text | pattern, scope?: Locator }
  | { kind: "textAbsent",  text | pattern }
  | { kind: "urlMatches",  pattern }
  | { kind: "all"|"any", of: Assertion[] }

OutcomeRule  { code: "MEMBER_NOT_FOUND", detect: Assertion, terminal: true,
               message, mapsTo: "business_outcome" }
RecoveryRule { id: "dismiss-system-notice", detect: Assertion,
               action: { click | wait | reauth | reload }, maxAttempts: 2 }
```

## Compilation (two phases — see also discovery-spec.mdc)

1. **Mechanical phase (no LLM):** every recorded action becomes a `Step`.
   `Locator.primary`/`fallbacks` come entirely from attributes captured off
   the real element at the moment it was acted on.
2. **Annotation phase (LLM, output must pass Zod validation):** the model
   only does four things — decide which literal values should become input
   parameters, write each step's `intent` text, propose the
   `successCheckpoint`, and propose candidate `outcomes`. On validation
   failure, retry once; on a second failure, fall back to the pure mechanical
   artifact with `status: "draft"`.

The compiled artifact always starts as `status: "draft"` and must be
explicitly approved (CLI or human) before unattended replay is allowed.
